<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anastasio Somoza García</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anastasio Somoza García was a Nicaraguan officer and dictator who, as director of the US-trained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a>, had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-cesar-sandino">Augusto César Sandino</a> murdered in 1934 and became president in 1937. He ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> directly or through puppet presidents until he was shot by the poet Rigoberto López Pérez in León in September 1956. He founded a family dynasty, continued by his sons Luis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-debayle">Anastasio Somoza Debayle</a>, that lasted until 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-garcia.jpg" alt="Anastasio Somoza García" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anastasio_Somoza_Garc%C3%ADa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anastasio Somoza García was born in San Marcos, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>'s Carazo Department, the son of a wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> planter. As a teenager he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, where he attended a business school and learned English. There he met Salvadora Debayle Sacasa, a member of one of Nicaragua's wealthiest families, whom he married in 1919. In 1926 he joined the Liberal rebellion in support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-sacasa">Juan Bautista Sacasa</a>, his wife's uncle, and, though he did not distinguish himself in battle, he acted as an interpreter in the United States-brokered negotiations. He then served as governor of León, consul to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> and foreign minister, and rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a>, the constabulary organised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps">US Marine Corps</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>When the Marines left in January 1933, Somoza was appointed director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a> at the urging of the US ambassador. During peace talks he ordered the killing of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-cesar-sandino">Augusto César Sandino</a> on 21 February 1934, in violation of a safe-conduct. In 1936 he used the Guard to force Sacasa to resign, was elected president in December and took office on 1 January 1937. He amended the constitution to centralise power, and family members and supporters held key posts in the government and military. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the state confiscated the properties of the German community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> and sold them to Somoza and his family at much-reduced prices; by 1944 he was the largest landowner in the country.</p><p>Under US pressure he agreed in 1944 to liberalise, legalise unions and not run in 1947. His chosen successor, Leonardo Argüello, tried to reduce the Guard's power, and less than a month after taking office in May 1947 Somoza arranged another coup. After the US government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry S. Truman</a> refused to recognise the new government, a Constituent Assembly named Víctor Manuel Román y Reyes president, and Somoza again became president in 1950 in a heavily rigged election. He expanded his business interests to include a national airline, textile mills and a container port.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1955 the constitution was amended so that he could run again. Shortly after his nomination he was shot in León on 21 September 1956 by the poet Rigoberto López Pérez, and he died at Gorgas Hospital in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal-zone">Panama Canal Zone</a>. His eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-somoza-debayle">Luis Somoza Debayle</a>, then speaker of the House, took over, and he and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-debayle">Anastasio Somoza Debayle</a> ruled for the next 23 years, directly or through figureheads. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> supported them as anti-communist stalwarts. Anastasio Somoza Debayle was forced to resign in 1979 and was assassinated in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> the following year. Somoza García is entombed with Luis in the National Guard Mausoleum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anastasio Somoza García” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The United States trained a new National Guard, which under <b>Anastasio Somoza García</b> became the pillar of the dictatorship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Marcos, in what is now <a href="/countries/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>. Died in Ancón, in what is now <a href="/countries/panama">Panama</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=11.91,-86.21&amp;year=1935" aria-label="San Marcos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2180 1720 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anastasio Somoza García"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2630 1990h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2630 1990h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=11.91,-86.21&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
